 ],
 "literal": "The great strong one of the land bordering the wilderness / has absolutely no wealth in hand; yet, desiring / the begging people who went to see him, / even if they ask for male elephants along with tall chariots, those are to be given / like when salt-merchants of salt-carrying carts point out / the hillock eroded by backwaters, / without any room for scorn is the wealth he thinks of giving.",
 "translation": "The lord of great strength, whose land borders the wilderness, has absolutely no wealth left in his hands. Yet, when supplicants go to him in hope, even if they ask for male elephants and tall chariots, he is duty-bound to give them. His giving is like a hillock eroded by backwaters, pointed out by salt-merchants driving salt-laden carts to dig up for earth; he lets himself be entirely depleted. The wealth he intends to give leaves no room for scorn.",
 "modern": "காட்டு வழியை ஒட்டிய நாட்டின் தலைவனான பெரும் வலிமை கொண்ட அவனிடம் கையில் பொருள் ஏதும் இல்லை. ஆயினும், அவனை நாடிச் சென்ற இரவலர்கள் யானைகளையும் பெரிய தேர்களையும் கேட்டாலும், அவற்றை அவர்களுக்குக் கொடுப்பது அவனது கடமையாகும். உப்பு ஏற்றிச் செல்லும் வண்டிகளை உடைய உமணர், சேற்று வழியை அடைப்பதற்காக உப்பங்கழியால் அரிக்கப்பட்ட குன்றைக் காட்டி அதிலிருந்து மண்ணை வெட்டி எடுப்பது போல, அவன் தன்னை முழுமையாக அழித்துக் கொடையளிப்பான். அவன் கொடுக்க நினைக்கும் கொடைப்பொருள் எள்ளளவும் இகழ்ச்சிக்கு இடமில்லாதது.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and Vallāṉ Mullai tuṟai, which extols the generous nature and valour of a warrior even in times of poverty. The poet Māṅkuṭi Marutaṉār praises a local chieftain who rules a dry, wilderness-bordering territory. Though the chieftain has no personal wealth left in his hands, he feels absolutely duty-bound to satisfy the grand requests of visiting supplicants, even if they ask for elephants and chariots. The poet uses a vivid dry-land image: salt-merchants driving heavy carts point to a coastal hillock eroded by backwaters to dig up its earth to pave their muddy path, eventually leveling the hillock. Similarly, the chieftain allows his own resources to be completely depleted by those in need, ensuring his reputation for unstinting generosity remains free from any scorn.",
